  { q: 'Are ravens common in the Pyrenees?', a: 'Yes — the common raven (Corvus corax) is a year-round resident of the Pyrenees, found throughout the mountain range from the Atlantic coast to the Mediterranean. The Pyrenees provide ideal raven habitat: rugged cliff faces for nesting, extensive pastoral land for foraging, and a large ungulate (sheep, cattle, wild boar, deer, chamois) population that provides carrion. Ravens are frequently seen soaring alongside griffon vultures and alongside bearded vultures (lammergeiers) at traditional carrion sites.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens nest on Pyrenean cliffs?', a: 'Ravens are cliff nesters throughout the Pyrenees, selecting inaccessible ledges on south-facing cliff faces for their bulky stick nests. Nesting begins in February — ravens are among the earliest breeding birds in the mountains, with eggs laid before snowmelt and chicks fledging in May–June. The same cliff sites are reused across years; some Pyrenean cliff sites show evidence of repeated raven nesting over decades. The timing of early breeding is linked to the raven\'s ability to exploit the first spring carrion resources.' },
  { q: 'What do ravens eat in the Pyrenees?', a: 'Pyrenean ravens are opportunistic omnivores with a diet centred on carrion (domestic livestock, wild ungulates, road-killed animals) supplemented by invertebrates, plant material, eggs, and small vertebrates. The Pyrenees\' extensive sheep and cattle transhumance traditions historically provided abundant livestock carcasses; modern continuation of pastoral farming maintains this resource. Ravens also exploit refuse from mountain refuges and tourist areas along the GR hiking routes.' },
];
